    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/taraz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taraz.jpg" alt="Taraz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Taraz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karakhan_Mausoleum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yakov Fedorov, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kara-Khanids, also called Karakhanids or Ilek Khanids, were a Turkic dynasty whose origins remain uncertain. Most historians connect them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluk</a> confederation, which had dominated the lands of the Chu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Talas</a> rivers since the eighth century, while others favour the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yaghma</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigil</a>, tribes that formed the core of their later armies. The khanate is conventionally dated from about 840, when the destruction of the Uyghur Khaganate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Kyrgyz</a> left a vacuum in the eastern steppe and probably allowed Karluk leaders to claim the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>. The early rulers, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> in the Chu valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, are known mainly from later Muslim traditions and from coins. In 893 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> amir Ismail ibn Ahmad captured Talas from them, showing that the Muslim states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> could still push the Turkic frontier back. The decisive change came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satuq-bughra-khan">Satuq Bughra Khan</a>, who according to tradition converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in Kashgar, perhaps around 934, with the help of Muslim merchants and scholars, and then seized power from his uncle. He died in 955, and under his successors Islam became the religion of the dynasty and of the tribes it led.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="Kashgar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%ABytgah_Mosque,_Kashi_(20230923100109).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N509FZ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kara-Khanid realm joined two different worlds: the pastures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Semirechye</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>, where Turkic tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yaghma</a> and Tukhsi herded horses and sheep, and the irrigated oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Shash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, with their Persian- and Sogdian-speaking farmers, craftsmen and merchants. The conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> lands in 999 gave the dynasty some of the richest cities of the Islamic east, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarqand</a>, whose textiles, paper and metalwork were traded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. The khanate sat astride the overland routes linking the Islamic world with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and caravans crossed it from Kashgar through the Ferghana valley to Samarqand. Large issues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins, struck in dozens of towns, reflect a monetised urban economy. Conversion drew the Turks into the religious life of the cities. The Kara-Khanids generally respected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> scholars of Bukhara and Samarqand, built mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, and endowed religious foundations, although relations between khans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> were sometimes tense. Among the nomads <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread more slowly, and older beliefs and customs persisted for generations. In the towns Turkic gradually joined Persian as a spoken language, a long process of Turkicisation that Kara-Khanid rule accelerated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314-15, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kara-Khanid power rested on tribal cavalry: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yaghma</a> and other tribes, led by members of the ruling clan and supplemented by the garrisons and militias of the oasis towns. Conversion gave the dynasty a cause for holy war against the Buddhist kingdom of Khotan, its eastern neighbour, and against non-Muslim Turks. The war against Khotan was long and hard, and verses preserved by Mahmud al-Kashgari recall raids on its towns and temples; Khotan finally fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-qadir-khan">Yusuf Qadir Khan</a>, probably around 1006. In the west the khanate exploited the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-bughra-khan">Hasan Bughra Khan</a> briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 992 but fell ill and died while withdrawing. In 999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasr-ibn-ali">Nasr ibn Ali</a>, the Ilek Khan, entered Bukhara and captured the last Samanid amir, Abd al-Malik II, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> took Khurasan. The two powers agreed to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> their frontier, but in 1006 Kara-Khanid armies invaded Khurasan while Mahmud was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Mahmud hurried back and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a disciplined professional army, defeated Nasr and Yusuf Qadir Khan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in January 1008. From the 1020s the Kara-Khanid princes fought increasingly among themselves, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turkmen</a> bands served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-tegin">Ali Tegin</a> of Bukhara in these wars.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/balasagun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balasagun.jpg" alt="Balasagun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burana_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bala Biott, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kara-Khanids ruled their lands as the collective property of the royal clan, in the tradition of the steppe empires. Supreme authority was divided between two great khans, a senior ruler with the title Arslan Khan ('lion khan') and a junior ruler with the title Bughra Khan ('<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> khan'), each supported by subordinate princes holding the titles <em>ilek</em> and <em>tegin</em>. Rank passed among the senior members of the dynasty, and princes held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>, often a city with its district, from which they could rise to higher titles. Numismatists have reconstructed much of this hierarchy from the coins, which name both the minting prince and his overlords. Two rival branches of the dynasty, conventionally named after their ancestors Ali and Hasan, competed for the senior titles, so the system bred rivalry as well as cooperation. The khans had no strong central bureaucracy of their own. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> they kept the Persian administrative traditions inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, relying on local officials, landowners (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihqan">dihqans</a></em>) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, who mediated between the Turkic rulers and the urban population. Grants of land revenue to princes and soldiers, of the kind later called <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em>, supported the army. Royal courts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzgen">Uzgen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarqand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> became centres of patronage for scholars and builders.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/burana-tower"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burana-tower.jpg" alt="Burana Tower" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/burana-tower">Burana Tower</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burana_Tower,_Kyrgyzstan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kara-Khanids were the first Turkic dynasty to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the religion of their state, and their conversion opened the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, from whom later Muslim Turkic dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> emerged. Their conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid</a> lands in 999 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> permanently under Turkic rule, beginning the transformation of the region's population and language that eventually made it part of Turkestan. Under Kara-Khanid patronage Turkic became, for the first time, a written language of Islamic literature. The most celebrated works were completed after the division of the khanate but grew out of its court culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Khass Hajib</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a></em> ('Wisdom of Royal Glory'), a mirror for princes in verse, around 1069–1070, and presented it to the khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>. Mahmud al-Kashgari, a prince of the dynasty, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan-lughat-al-turk">Diwan Lughat al-Turk</a></em>, a dictionary of the Turkic languages with a map of the Turkic world, in the 1070s. The dynasty's buildings, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burana-tower">Burana Tower</a> near Balasagun, the mausoleums of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzgen">Uzgen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-minaret">Kalyan Minaret</a> raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1127 by the western Kara-Khanids, shaped the later architecture of Central Asia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan.jpg" alt="Battle of Dandanaqan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaznaly-seljuk_Dandanaqan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The united khanate did not survive the rivalries built into its system of collective rule. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasr-ibn-ali">Nasr ibn Ali</a> in 1012 or 1013, princes of the two branches fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-tegin">Ali Tegin</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> around 1020, and in 1025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> met the eastern khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-qadir-khan">Yusuf Qadir Khan</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarqand</a> and agreed to act against him, although no lasting settlement followed. Yusuf Qadir Khan died in 1032 and Ali Tegin in 1034, and their heirs struggled to hold their lands as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turkmen</a> grew powerful in the region. Around 1040, as the Seljuks defeated the Ghaznavids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-tamghach-khan">Ibrahim ibn Nasr</a>, known as Böritegin and later as Tamghach Khan, took control of Bukhara and Samarqand. By about 1042 the realm had split into two independent khanates: a western one centred on Samarqand and Bukhara and an eastern one centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> contested between them. Both survived for more than a century and a half. The Western Kara-Khanids became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Seljuks after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> took Samarqand in 1089, and both branches later submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qatwan">Battle of Qatwan</a> in 1141. The eastern line ended around 1211, and the western in 1212, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii">Khwarazmshah Muhammad II</a> executed the last khan of Samarqand.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kara-Khanid Khanate was the first Turkic state to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as its religion, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> under lasting Turkic rule with the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 999, and nurtured the beginnings of Turkic Islamic literature, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutadgu-bilig">Kutadgu Bilig</a> and Mahmud al-Kashgari's great dictionary emerged.</em></p>

